  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1113",
      "html": "<p>Amazon, Upper Amazon, Nanay. Photographed in the Rio Nanay near Iquitos, Peru.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/pseudopimelodidae/batrochoglanis_villosus.htm",
      "html": "<p>South America: Demerara River, Essequibo basin, Orinoco, and Amazon River basins. Type locality: Potaro Landing, British Guiana.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/pseudopimelodidae/batrochoglanis_villosus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Provide plenty of hiding places to hide during the day.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/pseudopimelodidae/batrochoglanis_villosus.htm",
      "html": "<p>No problem with eating all types of aquarium fare: flake, tablets, pellets, live and frozen foods.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/pseudopimelodidae/batrochoglanis_villosus.htm",
      "html": "<p>This genus is predatory in the aquarium towards fish that are smaller. Best kept in a small group of 2-3. Very secretive in its surroundings.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1113",
      "html": "<p>Breeding: unreported. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1113",
      "html": "<p>IUCN Red List category: Not Evaluated. A juvenile of 3-4 cm SL was sold as Microglanis.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/pseudopimelodidae/batrochoglanis_villosus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: the specific name villosus is Latin for hairy or shaggy, referring to fine hair-like filaments over the entire body, especially abundant above the pectoral fin.</p><p>IUCN Red List: despite the limited information available on this species, it is assessed as Least Concern due to its wide range (IUCN 2022).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/pseudopimelodidae/batrochoglanis_villosus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Brejão, G.L. 2022. Batrochoglanis villosus. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2022.</p><p>Ferraris, C.J. Jr., 2007. Checklist of catfishes, recent and fossil (Osteichthyes: Siluriformes), and catalogue of siluriform primary types. Zootaxa 1418:1-628.</p><p>Froese, R. and D. Pauly. Editors. 2019. FishBase. www.fishbase.org (11/2019).</p><p>Shibatta, O.A., 2003. Pseudopimelodidae (Bumblebee catfishes, dwarf marbled catfishes). p. 401-405. In R.E. Reis, S.O. Kullander and C.J. Ferraris, Jr. (eds.) Checklist of the Freshwater Fishes of South and Central America. Porto Alegre: EDIPUCRS, Brasil.</p><p>The ETYFish Project Fish Name Etymology Database © Christopher Scharpf.</p>"
    }
